{
  "title": "Pangea™ Proximal Humerus Plate",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/implants/423193/pangea-tm--proximal-humerus-plate",
  "subtitle": "Proximal Humerus Fractures Updated: Feb 27 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Design",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Variable Angle Locking Technology**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423193/images/089e90d6-a14e-4b89-abf1-33d9b653b60d_screenshot_2025-01-07_at_11.01.30_am.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Pangea’s variable angle locking technology uses a CoCr locking screw, which is harder than the Ti6Al4V ELI plate, allowing for the screwhead’s threads to form a definitive plate-screw interface in the plate's locking hole by engaging the softer, Ti6Al4V material.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "This technology allows the user to aim and lock the screw into the plate within a true 30° cone of the predetermined hole trajectory.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "The variable angle drill guide provided with the system offers guidance with respect to the limit of the 30° cone.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "The locking mechanism remains functional for up to three attempts at locking screw insertion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Evidence-based Screw Trajectories**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Proximal Screw Row Incorporates Cross-Diverging Screw Trajectories",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "The most proximal screw row creates a lattice that is designed to decrease the likelihood of posterior superior screw penetration and cutout.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**7 Beveled and Angled 2.0mm Suture Hole Cutouts**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Provides flexibility in tendon and tuberosity fixation and to facilitate easier passage of suture after the plate is placed on bone.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423193/images/f00dff52-43c1-42b9-ac73-cc5a20221134_screenshot_2025-01-08_at_10.44.46_am.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**3 Variable Angle Calcar Screw Holes**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Provides additional flexibility to achieve screw placement in the calcar bone of varying fracture patterns and patient morphologies.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Waisted Scalloped Shape**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Reduced potential for periosteal disturbance while maintaining strength of plate and final construct.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423193/images/f00dff52-43c1-42b9-ac73-cc5a20221134_screenshot_2025-01-08_at_10.44.46_am.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Related Implants",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea™ series contains 3 humerus plates",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Proximal Humerus (this topic)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Proximal Humerus Posterior**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Extra Articular Distal Humerus**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Indications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea Humerus Plating System is indicated for the internal fixation and stabilization of humerus bone fractures and osteotomies in normal and osteopenic bone, including",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Diaphyseal fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Metaphyseal fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Epiphyseal fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Extra-articular fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Intra-articular fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Periprosthetic fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Non-unions",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Malunions",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Deformities",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Placement",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Correct calcar screw placement should be the primary driver of plate placement, but it is recommended that the plate sits 5-10mm distal to the posterior aspect of the greater tuberosity.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The plate is positioned directly lateral to the bicipital groove which can act as a reference landmark for plate placement.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Helps to ensure the positioning lateral and parallel to the bicipital groove.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fit",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Using the Stryker Orthopaedics Modeling and Analytics (SOMA) database, Pangea offers an evidence-based, anatomically informed implant",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The Pangea Proximal Humerus Plate provides better anatomic fit compared to the PHILOS plate from Synthes.1",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Approaches",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Surgeons may use a standard deltopectoral or deltoid split approach to the proximal humerus",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
